# Line endings for the files that are executed on a Linux host.
#
# The repository already stores these as LF, so a `git clone` on the server is
# correct today - but only because of how git is configured, not because
# anything says so. This machine has core.autocrlf=true, which means the
# Windows working tree holds CRLF. A technician who copies the working tree to
# the server with scp or WinSCP instead of pulling gets scripts that fail with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M", and a systemd unit whose ExecStart line
# continuation may not parse.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working tree LF as well, on every platform, so the file on
# disk is the file that runs wherever it came from.
#
# Scoped deliberately to executable and unit files. A blanket `* text=auto`
# would renormalise the whole repository in one commit and bury real changes.

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.timer         text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Container and compose files are read by Linux tooling.
Containerfile   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to translate anything inside them.
*.sql.gz        binary
*.tar           binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
